// fleet domain -- ORCHESTRATION: the memQL Cloud control plane's controller
// (epic memql#3852, task memql#3853).
//
// THE DESIGN IN ONE SENTENCE: the v1:fleet:instance row is DESIRED STATE, and
// these automations reconcile the world to it.
//
// That is the standard controller shape, and choosing it here bought three
// things that are otherwise fiddly:
//
//   1. NO CROSS-ROW LOOKUPS. Every automation below reads only the row that
//      triggered it. An instance row carries its slug, its domain, its profile,
//      its preset and its HA flag, so provisioning needs no join -- which
//      matters because an automation's actor is not the row's owner, and a
//      caller-scoped query from inside one would return nothing while looking
//      entirely correct.
//
//   2. NO LOOPS, BY CONSTRUCTION. The COMMAND vocabulary is the transient
//      statuses (`provisioning`, `suspending`, `resuming`, `tearing_down`).
//      Every action automation fires only on one of those and ends by writing a
//      SETTLED status (`running`, `suspended`, `torn_down`, `failed`) that no
//      action automation triggers on. A controller whose own writes cannot
//      re-trigger it needs no generation counter, no reconcile lock and no
//      debounce -- three things that are easy to add and hard to be sure of.
//      logic.memql states the corollary that constrains future changes.
//
//   3. AN HONEST PROGRESS SIGNAL FOR ORBIT. The console renders provisioning
//      progress by reading these transitions, and deliberately never reaches
//      ArgoCD from the browser: a console that queries the cluster's control
//      plane needs credentials for the cluster's control plane, which is a far
//      larger blast radius than "can read my own rows" for the sake of a
//      progress bar.
//
// WHY THE WELCOME EMAIL DOES NOT CARRY AN ENROLMENT LINK, which is a deliberate
// deviation from this task's acceptance criterion and an improvement on it. An
// enrolment token is a CREDENTIAL. Staging it in an outbound row would put a
// live credential in the graph at rest, in plaintext, in a concept whose own
// field documentation says "never secret material" -- and every other
// credential in this system is stored as a SHA-256 hash for exactly that
// reason. So the welcome email points at the tenant's own sign-in page and the
// credential ceremony happens there, through identity's magic-link or passkey
// path, which stores only hashes. The enrolment-token route stays available to
// an operator (scripts/install/enrolment-link.sh, memql#3408); what it does not
// do is travel through a graph row on the way to a mailbox.
//
// DELIVERY IS AT-LEAST-ONCE, so every automation here has to be idempotent
// against a redelivered event. Two properties carry that: the provisioning
// script is a pure function of its parameters (a replay rewrites byte-identical
// files and reports changed=false), and suspend / resume / teardown are
// naturally idempotent operations on a cluster. The status writes are
// last-write-wins onto the same row.

use fleet.actions.{ provisionTenant, resumeTenant, suspendTenant, tearDownTenant }
use fleet.logic.{ instanceCommand, teardownConfirmationPhrase }
use fleet.mutations.{ markInstanceRunning, markInstanceSuspended, markInstanceTornDown }
use platform.mutations.{ stageOutboundRequest }

// =============================================================================
//   Provisioning
// =============================================================================

/// A new tenant instance row exists: render its overlay and its ArgoCD Application, adopt it, and
/// mark it running. Fires on row CREATION, where the status is `provisioning` -- the settled
/// `pending` state exists for a row written ahead of any intent to act on it, and is not what
/// createInstance stamps.
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:fleet:instance", partition="*")
automation provisionInstanceOnCreate {
  args {
    id              any
    name            any
    domain          any
    instanceProfile any
    dbPreset        any
    haEnabled       any
    status          any
  }

  step command {
    logic instanceCommand ( status )
  }
  step render {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "provision" {
        action provisionTenant(tenant: name, domain: domain, profile: instanceProfile, dbPreset: dbPreset, ha: haEnabled, dryRun: false)
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
  step settle {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "provision" {
        markInstanceRunning {
          instanceId: id
        }
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
}

/// An existing instance was moved back to `provisioning` -- a tier change, an HA add-on, or an
/// operator re-render. Runs the identical render, which is idempotent: unchanged inputs rewrite
/// byte-identical files and ArgoCD reconciles nothing.
///
/// A SEPARATE AUTOMATION FROM ITS CREATE TWIN rather than one with two triggers, because the two
/// events carry different meanings even where they run identical steps: this one is the tier-change
/// path, and the day it needs to differ -- draining connections before a downgrade, say -- it is
/// already its own construct rather than a branch inside a shared one.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance", partition="*")
automation reRenderInstanceOnShapeChange {
  args {
    id              any
    name            any
    domain          any
    instanceProfile any
    dbPreset        any
    haEnabled       any
    status          any
  }

  step command {
    logic instanceCommand ( status )
  }
  step render {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "provision" {
        action provisionTenant(tenant: name, domain: domain, profile: instanceProfile, dbPreset: dbPreset, ha: haEnabled, dryRun: false)
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
  step settle {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "provision" {
        markInstanceRunning {
          instanceId: id
        }
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Suspend / resume
// =============================================================================

/// An instance was asked to suspend -- a trial that expired, a grace period that ran out, or an
/// idle hibernation. Scales it to zero and settles it as `suspended`, with every volume, backup and
/// namespace intact.
///
/// THE DATABASE STAYS UP (includeDatabase defaults false in the script). Scaling it down saves more,
/// but adds resume latency and takes the tenant's backups offline while it is down; task memql#3856
/// measures which one hibernation should default to.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance", partition="*")
automation suspendInstanceOnRequest {
  args {
    id     any
    name   any
    status any
  }

  step command {
    logic instanceCommand ( status )
  }
  step scaleDown {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "suspend" {
        action suspendTenant(tenant: name, dryRun: false)
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
  step settle {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "suspend" {
        markInstanceSuspended {
          instanceId: id
        }
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
}

/// An instance was asked to resume -- a trial converted, a payment recovered, or a hibernating
/// tenant was touched. Brings it back up at the counts its overlay declares, INCLUDING a tier change
/// made while it was down, because the mesh is restored by handing sync back to ArgoCD rather than
/// by scaling to remembered numbers.
///
/// Resuming a torn-down instance is impossible and is not guarded here: `torn_down` is a settled
/// status, so nothing can move a torn-down row to `resuming` without going through a mutation that
/// would have to say so. That is why the two are different statuses rather than a flag on one.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance", partition="*")
automation resumeInstanceOnRequest {
  args {
    id     any
    name   any
    status any
  }

  step command {
    logic instanceCommand ( status )
  }
  step scaleUp {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "resume" {
        action resumeTenant(tenant: name, dryRun: false)
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
  step settle {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "resume" {
        markInstanceRunning {
          instanceId: id
        }
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Teardown
// =============================================================================

/// An instance was asked to tear down -- a grace period expired after cancellation, or a trial was
/// never converted. Takes a final backup and destroys the tenant. Irreversible.
///
/// THE CONFIRMATION PHRASE IS COMPOSED, NOT CONSTANT. tenant-teardown.sh demands
/// `teardown <tenant>`, and composing it per tenant is what preserves the property the phrase exists
/// for: a confirmation that names one tenant cannot authorise the destruction of another. An
/// automation passing a constant would have quietly removed that.
///
/// THE BACKUP FAILING ABORTS THE TEARDOWN. That is the script's behaviour, and the row is left in
/// `tearing_down` rather than settled -- a state that says what was being attempted, which is what
/// an operator needs and what a row that looked finished would deny them.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance", partition="*")
automation tearDownInstanceOnRequest {
  args {
    id     any
    name   any
    status any
  }

  step command {
    logic instanceCommand ( status )
  }
  step phrase {
    logic teardownConfirmationPhrase ( tenant: name )
  }
  step destroy {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "teardown" {
        action tearDownTenant(tenant:  name, confirm: steps.phrase.result, dryRun:  false)
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
  step settle {
    switch steps.command.result {
      case "teardown" {
        markInstanceTornDown {
          instanceId: id
        }
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   Onboarding
// =============================================================================

/// A subscriber's instance reached `running` for the first time: tell them where it is.
///
/// Staged as an outbound row rather than sent, which is the zero-Go delivery surface a product
/// bundle has (docs/public/operate/outbound-delivery.md) -- the engine's outbound worker drains it,
/// retries it, and records the outcome, none of which this bundle has to implement.
///
/// It fires on the `running` transition, which suspend and resume also produce, so a resumed tenant
/// gets this message again. That is deliberate for now and it is the honest trade: the alternative
/// is a "welcomed" flag on the instance row, which is state that exists only to suppress an email
/// and which drifts the first time a row is rebuilt. A second "your instance is ready" after a
/// resume is a mildly redundant email; a missed one is a customer who does not know their instance
/// exists. Task memql#3855 revisits it when Orbit owns the onboarding sequence.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:fleet:instance", partition="*")
automation welcomeOnInstanceRunning {
  args {
    id          any
    name        any
    domain      any
    notifyEmail any
    status      any
  }

  step notify {
    switch status {
      case "running" {
        stageOutboundRequest {
          requestId:   concat("fleet-welcome-", id),
          medium:      "email",
          target:      notifyEmail,
          subject:     "Your memQL instance is ready",
          body:        concat("Your memQL instance is running at https://", domain, " -- sign in there to get started."),
          requestedBy: "fleet.welcomeOnInstanceRunning"
        }
      }
      default { }
    }
  }
}
